---
title: "The multi-page subsystem"
description: "What the page walker collects, how a book differs from a website, and the projections built on top: search and cross-references."
---

A directory with a `_site.yml` is a **project**: a website or a book. The core's
`site/` module owns everything the
single-page path never had, the project-level concerns, while the per-page server
that drives it lives in [the dev server](server.tmd). This chapter is the core
subsystem: discovery, the config model, the front-matter primitives, link
rewriting, cross-page references, and search.

```{mermaid}
%%| label: fig-site-render
%%| fig-cap: "A directory is discovered once; each page then runs through the shared core pipeline plus the site-only steps."
flowchart TB
  dir[("directory:<br/>_site.yml + .tmd pages")] --> disc["Site::discover<br/>config · pages · order · xref registry · search index"]
  disc --> page["per page · render_page"]
  page --> core["core pipeline<br/>(.tmd → blocks → HTML)"]
  core --> fm["expand front-matter blocks<br/>hero: / listing:"]
  fm --> chrome["wrap in chrome<br/>navbar / footer / chapter drawer / prev-next"]
  chrome --> links["rewrite .tmd→.html<br/>+ resolve cross-page xrefs"]
  links --> out["page HTML"]
```

## Discovery

Drop `post.tmd` into a `posts/` folder beside a `_site.yml` and it joins a
website. `Site::discover` reads the root `_site.yml`, then walks the directory for
input pages: every `.tmd` becomes a page, **skipping `_`-prefixed directories**
(partials, `_freeze`, `_site`) and dotfiles. Each page maps to its output URL (`x.tmd` →
`x.html`, mirroring the input tree). Both `build` (static) and `preview` (live) drive a
project through the same `discover` + `render_page`.

One kind of `.tmd` is discovered but deliberately kept out of the navigable page set: a
`draft: true` page is held out of the **published** view (no output, nav, listing, or
prev/next entry).

### How `draft:` filtering works {#sec-draft-filtering}

`draft:` is resolved by a **discovery mode**, so the same project yields two different
page sets:

| Mode | Used by | `draft: true` pages |
|---|---|---|
| `DraftMode::Exclude` | `build`, `build --check-only` (plain `Site::discover`) | dropped from `self.pages`, recorded in `Site::excluded_drafts` |
| `DraftMode::Include` | `preview` (`Site::discover_with`) | kept, tagged `Page.draft`, badged in the view |

The exclusion is structural, not cosmetic: in `Exclude`, `website_pages` returns `None`
*before* the file ever becomes a `Page`, so the draft never enters `self.pages` — the one
list every downstream feature reads from. Because listings, prev/next navigation, the
navbar, the search index, and the xref registry all derive from `self.pages`, a draft
drops out of every one of them at once, and `build` emits no file for it. In `Include` the
draft *is* a `Page`, so it flows through that same machinery and appears in listings and
nav in context, carrying `draft: true` so the view can badge it.

Making `Exclude` the zero-argument default (`Site::discover`) is deliberate: the worst
failure is a draft leaking into a build, so every consumer is safe unless it explicitly
opts into `Include`. Only the preview does.

**Books consult `draft:` too.** A book's page set comes from the explicit `chapters:`
list, so the gate lives in `build_book`: an `Exclude` draft chapter is skipped without
bumping the chapter counter, so the book renumbers as if it were never listed and the
drawer never links a page that wasn't built; a part whose chapters are *all* drafts drops
its now-empty header. In `Include` the chapter stays, numbered in context and marked in
the drawer. (Leaving a file out of `chapters:` still works as before.)

## The config model

The typed `SiteConfig` is Taliesin's **native, flat** schema (title, nav, footer,
`mounts:`, ...). Config is deliberately **two levels with no cascade**: the root
`_site.yml` sets project-wide defaults and a page's own front matter overrides
them, full stop. There is no `_metadata.yml` directory merge, the single most
confusing part of the older model.

The schema is a **closed set**: an unknown top-level key warns with a "did you
mean" hint (`config/mod.rs` `validate_keys`). A legacy nested config
(`project:` / `website:` / `book:` / `format:`) is no longer accepted: those keys
warn as unknown and their nested values are ignored, so such a config must be
flattened to the native shape.

`validate_keys` is deliberately a **warning, not an error**: every top-level key in
`_site.yml` is checked against the `NATIVE_KEYS` constant (`title`, `author`,
`description`, `url`, `favicon`, `logo`, `head`, `nav`, `footer`, `chapters`, `mounts`,
`python`, `r`, `bibliography`), and an unknown one is reported but the rest of
the config still parses. A key the config *used* to honour is answered from
`frontmatter::RETIRED_KEYS` under the `config key` scope, so it gets the reason it went
and what to write instead rather than a rename hint. Otherwise the "did you mean" comes
from the same `closest()` the
front-matter linter and the CLI's unknown-command suggestion use: the nearest known key
within **Levenshtein edit distance 2** (so `favicn` suggests `favicon`, but a key two-plus
edits away suggests nothing rather than guessing wildly). Two harder failures also degrade
gracefully rather than aborting: a missing `_site.yml` and a YAML file that does not parse
both warn and fall back to a `SiteConfig::default()`, so the lint and `preview` still run
and surface the problem instead of crashing.

::: {.callout-note}
The closed set is the *whole* contract for `_site.yml`: there is no second tier that
tolerates unknown keys. This is what lets `schema.rs` generate a JSON Schema directly
from `NATIVE_KEYS`, the very same list `validate_keys` checks against, so the editor
autocomplete and the runtime validator can never drift apart.
:::

Taliesin generates a JSON Schema for `_site.yml` from `NATIVE_KEYS` (the same
closed set the config validator uses), and the VS Code companion ships a copy of it. See the
Configuration reference (`../guide/reference/configuration.html`) for the editor
autocomplete on-ramp.

## Books vs websites

The same machinery produces both, branched on one key: **`chapters:` present ⇒ a
book** (one centred reading column; the chapter list lives in an off-canvas drawer
summoned from a slim sticky topbar, with numbered chapters + sections and
prev/next-*chapter* navigation), otherwise a website (a top navbar). `book.rs` computes
the section numbers that the chapter list and cross-references display. No `type:` is
needed; the shape of the config decides. A `chapters:` entry is a bare path, a
`{ part:, chapters: }` group, or a `{ file:, text: }` mapping whose `text:`
overrides the chapter label (otherwise the label is the chapter's first `# H1`).

The drawer's markup is a flat chapter list, with no further client-side hydration.

::: {.callout-note}
## A chapter's **nav label** and its **page title** are different things

A book chapter's label — in the drawer and the Cmd-K outline —
resolves `text:` → first `# H1` → front-matter `title:` → the file stem. Its `<title>`
and social metadata come from the document's own `title:`. So a chapter that sets both
a `title:` and a different opening `# H1` deliberately shows two names, and that is the
supported way to give a chapter a short nav label and a longer page title (the guide's
preface is titled "Taliesin" and opens `# Why Taliesin`).

The rule differs from a **website** page, whose `Page.title` resolves `title:` → `# H1`,
title-first. That asymmetry is deliberate: a website page has no nav label distinct from
its title, and a book chapter does. Measured across every book in the repo, 3 of 48
chapters currently differ, all of them on purpose.
:::

## Front-matter primitives

Two front-matter blocks turn a page into something structured, and both
render into the **framework's own primitives with no bespoke HTML**, the same
primitives the marketing site is built from:

- **`hero:`** replaces the title block with the `.hero` band (eyebrow, headline,
  lead, CTA buttons).
- **`listing:`** crawls a `contents:` directory and emits a card per document (image,
  date, title, description, categories), sorted by date and capped, as a grid or a
  stacked list, so a blog index or a projects page generates from front matter.

## Cross-page links and references

Two related rewrites stitch the pages together:

- **Link rewriting.** Intra-site `.tmd` links (including `other.tmd#anchor`) are
  rewritten to their built `.html` targets, so the source links the way you'd type
  it and the output links correctly.
- **Cross-page cross-references** (`xref.rs`). A project-wide registry scans every
  page for `{#sec-}` / `{#fig-}` / … anchors (plus a section number for numbered book
  sections), then rewrites `data-tali-xref`-marked links to point at the right page.
  So a `@fig-` in one chapter resolves, with its number, to its figure in another.

The cross-page resolution is a deliberate **three pass** design, because a `@fig-`
reference can point at a label defined on a page that has not been rendered yet:

1. **Scan all pages first.** `scan_xref_targets` walks every page's *source* (a
   lightweight pass, no render, no execution) and records the anchors it can see from the
   source alone into a `HashMap<label, XrefTarget>`. The recognized prefixes are `sec-`,
   `fig-`, `tbl-`, `eq-`, `lst-`, `thm-`, `lem-`, `cor-`, `prp-`, `def-`, `exm-`, and
   `rem-` (`is_ref_anchor` is the list; `cite::XREF_LABELS` is pinned against it so the
   two cannot drift). Each `XrefTarget` stores the anchor's page URL and, for a numbered
   book section, its section number. Includes are resolved during this scan exactly as the
   render pipeline resolves them, so section counters advance over included headings and
   the numbers match the rendered page. **First definition wins** project-wide; if a
   *different* page redefines the same label, it warns once (a within-page duplicate is
   caught separately by the per-page render).

   What this pass **cannot** see: it reads `{#fig-x}` brace ids and skips fenced code, so
   a float labelled by a *cell directive* (`#| label: fig-x`, `%%| label: fig-x`) is
   invisible to it twice over — the label lives inside a fence, and it is not a brace id.
   Numbers are invisible too: a figure/equation/table/listing number is assigned
   only during render, so the scan leaves it empty.

2. **Render-harvest.** `Site::harvest_xref_numbers` renders every page once (scoped to its
   chapter, no code execution) and reads the renderer's own registry, `doc.xref_numbers`.
   This fills in the numbers pass 1 left empty — so a cross-page `@fig-`/`@tbl-` reads
   "Figure&nbsp;2.3" / "Table&nbsp;2.1" rather than a bare label — and **inserts** the
   anchors pass 1 could not see at all, which is how a cell-labelled float becomes a
   cross-page target. Reusing the render's registry (rather than teaching the scan to
   parse cell options) keeps one source of truth for *which fences are cells*, so the two
   cannot drift; the same reason `brace_id` parses with the renderer's own `parse_attrs`.
   The registry is looser than the scan in one way, so the harvest re-applies
   `is_ref_anchor`: a Markdown-table caption registers any id, and a `{#my-table}` that no
   `@`-reference can reach must not be advertised as a target. It runs once from
   `discover`, so `build`, `preview`, and the lint all resolve identically.

3. **Rewrite during page render.** When a page is rendered, `cite::process` has already
   turned each `@sec-` reference into a bare-label link that carries a `data-tali-xref`
   marker naming the anchor:

   ```html
   <a href="#sec-intro" data-tali-xref="sec-intro">Section</a>
   ```

   `rewrite_cross_refs` then scans that HTML for those markers: a marker whose anchor
   resolves to a *different* page is rewritten to `href="<up><page>#anchor"` with its
   number folded in (and a chapter-level `@sec-` reference reads "Chapter N"),
   while a same-page or unknown anchor is left exactly as `cite` emitted it. So the
   local case and the cross-page case share one syntax and the reader never sees the seam.

## Full-text search

`search.rs` builds **one cross-page index**: every page's title and each anchored
heading, each carrying the **plain-text body of its section**, so Cmd-K matches
prose, not just headings. It is built once at discovery and **written to a separate
`search-index.js`, lazy-loaded by the client on first open**, rather than inlined into
every page, so the index never bloats page weight. (On a 17-page book that saved
over a megabyte across the pages.) The palette is opened by the navbar search
control (or the book topbar's, for a book) or the <kbd>Cmd/Ctrl-K</kbd> shortcut.

`page_fragment` renders each page's Markdown once (no code execution, so the anchor
ids match the served pages exactly), then emits a flat JSON array of compact records, one
per searchable target. Each record is `{u, p, i, l, t, b}` plus two optional fields: the
page **u**rl, the **p**age title, the anchor **i**d, the heading **l**evel, the heading
**t**ext, the **b**ody text of that section, the book **c**hapter number, and the
ancestor **h**eading path. Every page contributes one record for the page itself (id `""`,
level `0`, body = the intro before the first heading) plus one record per anchored heading
(body = the plain text from that heading's close to the next heading's open). The section
body is capped at 1500 characters so one long section or a big code listing cannot blow up
the index, and the author's own `404.html` is skipped so a search never surfaces it.

`c` and `h` are omitted where they are empty, so a website's index is byte-identical to
one built without them. They exist because the palette renders the index as the *book's
outline*, not a flat row list: `c` numbers the chapter row (a page-title record carries
the bare title, since the rendered section numbers live on headings), and `h` says where a
section sits inside its chapter.

The page's blocks are finished in the **same order the served page finishes them**
(`Site::finish_blocks`: number the chapter, then resolve cross-refs). Scoping the render
numbers floats but *not* headings — that is `number_chapter_headings` — so
without that call every heading indexes unnumbered while the page it points at reads "5.2
How nulls behave", and a reader cannot search the number they can see.

A page that emits no title block and opens at `# H1` has that heading as its **own title**,
not a section, so it is folded into the page record rather than indexed separately: it is
the same destination, one line below, reading the same words. (The titled shape already
behaves this way, since a title block's text lands in the page record's body.)

Search is entirely **in-browser**: there is no server query endpoint. The client loads
`search-index.js` once (via `window.TALIESIN_SEARCH_URL`, set in the page head) the first
time the palette opens, caches the array on `window.TALIESIN_SEARCH_INDEX`, and filters it
locally as you type. It is loaded as a `<script>` element rather than fetched, so it works
under `file://` too. A static `build` ships the same `search-index.js` beside the pages, so
search works on a deployed, server-less site identically to the live preview.

::: {.callout-note}
The index is built from `self.pages`, so the draft exclusion compounds automatically: in
a published build a `draft:` page is absent from `self.pages`, and therefore absent from
search, without search needing any special case for it. (In
`preview` a draft *is* in `self.pages`, so it is searchable while you work on it — the
same mechanism, read through the other discovery mode. See @sec-draft-filtering.)
:::

## Word counts

Word counts come from `prose::word_count` rather than from the rendered text, so the book
nav, the LSP outline's `detail` and the page's own reading-time figure cannot report
different lengths. The LSP outline carries a per-*section* count where the nav does not,
because `lsp_outline` computed the line extent already — and because a node's extent spans
its subsections, a parent there says "N words total" while a leaf says "N words".

## Social & SEO metadata

Each page emits five tags and no more (`meta.rs`): `og:title`, `og:description`, `og:url`,
`og:image` and `twitter:card`, plus `<meta name="description">` because that is what a
search result reads. Titles and descriptions come from the page's own front matter and fall
back to the `_site.yml` config; `og:image` is the page's own `image:`, absolutized.

`og:url` and `og:image` are emitted **only when a `url:` is configured**, since a social
scraper needs absolute URLs, and `twitter:card` degrades from `summary_large_image` to
`summary` when there is no image — which is the right shape for a link with no picture, not
a defect. The 404 page advertises no image, matching its exclusion from the sitemap.

**This was reduced on 2026-08-08**, from a block that also carried `og:type`,
`og:site_name`, `og:image:width`/`height`, `twitter:title`/`description`/`image`,
`<link rel="canonical">` and a schema.org JSON-LD `@graph` (`BlogPosting`, upgraded to
`ScholarlyArticle` for a cited post; `WebSite` + `Person` on the root index). The
per-page `og:image` used to be a **generated** 1200x630 PNG, rasterized from a bundled
Newsreader TTF at build time and written to `og/<hash>.png`; the page's own `image:` was
deliberately excluded from it. Now it is the only source, so nothing here is generated and
nothing here can drift from a file on disk.

## Mounts

`mounts:` serves *another* project under a URL prefix in `preview`, which is how the
marketing site resolves its `/docs/guide` and `/docs/internals` links live. Under
`preview` a mounted page is **rendered on request as static output**, so it has no live
websocket, no dev menu, and no click-to-source; to edit a mounted book with the full live
loop, preview it directly (`preview docs/internals`) rather than through the mount.

The static `build` wires mounts too: after finishing its own pages it runs a **full,
independent build of each mounted project** into `<out>/<at>/`, so the deployed
`/docs/internals/` links resolve exactly as the previewed ones do. One command builds the
whole tree.

This is a recursion, not an inheritance. Each mount is built by the same code path as a
top-level `build`, reading its own `_site.yml`, its own theme, its own execution settings —
nothing cascades down from the parent, which is what the flat, no-cascade config model is
for. The parent contributes exactly one thing: the output directory, `<out>/<at>/`, which is
the URL prefix the parent already committed to in its nav. `--strict` and the kernel
requirement propagate, because a mount is part of the artifact being written.

For a while `build` stopped at a warning here, on the argument that a separate project
deserves an explicit command. What that produced in practice was a deploy whose primary call
to action 404'd, a diagnostic (`TAL-MOUNT-PREVIEW`) written to describe the hole, and a shell
script beside the site to fill it — three artifacts standing in for one recursion. A config
key that works in `preview` and not in `build` is a bug with a rationale attached.

**The order is load-bearing.** The parent is built first and the mounts second, because the
parent's stale-output sweep deletes everything under the output directory it did not itself
write, and `_site/docs/guide/` is not one of the dot-, underscore- or symlink-prefixed
exemptions. A mount built first would be swept away by the parent, silently, on a green
exit. The sweep is deliberately *not* taught to skip mount prefixes: sweeping them is what
makes a mount deleted from `_site.yml` disappear from the deploy, and the cost of the
delete-and-rewrite is bounded because `_freeze/` means no cell re-executes.

A mount that resolves outside its containment boundary is not built, for the same reason it
is not served: `load_config` has already refused it, and building it would perform the
escape one step later.

::: {.callout-tip}
Because a mounted page under `preview` is rendered as plain static output (no websocket, no
click-to-source), preview a mounted book *directly* (`preview docs/internals`) when you
want the full incremental live loop on it, and use the mount only to check that the
cross-project links resolve.
:::
